Subclasses: ExpressionBinary, ExpressionUnary, ExpressionValue

Responsibilies:
Perform binary operations on two expressionscollaborates with: ExpressionBinary
evaluate the current value (provided it's a constant expression)
I can answer the symbol of the operation I represent
answer my value (how is this different from evaluate?)
Answer an instance of myself created from a string representation
Answer the string representation of the expression I represent
Answer my symbolic derivative
Services: *, +, -, /, abs, derivative, evaluateAt:, negated, operation, operationString, raisedTo:To just use Expression, you will never need to know the names of any of the subclasses, or how to use the subclasses.  The Expression instance creation method knows about all of its subclasses (hard coded, but easily modifiable), and builds an expression tree	To instantiate an Expression, use one of the following:		Expression newFromString: 'x^7'		'x^7' asExpressionEXAMPLES:	(use alt+p to see the results for yourself)	'(1+32*x-7/x)' asExpression evaluateAt: ({ #x->7 } as: Dictionary)		224	'(1+32*x-7/x)' asExpression evaluateAt: 7		224	'(1 + 32*x - 7/y + cos(ln(tan((z^75*14/w)^0.5))))' asExpression evaluateAt: ({ #x->7. #y->42. #z->4. #w->0.1212121 } as: Dictionary)		225.8333333333333	'(1 + 32*x - 7/y + cos(ln(tan((z^75*14/w)^0.5))))' asExpression evaluateAt: 42.73		1369.196180669319	'(1+32*x-7/x)' asExpression derivativeWithRespectTo: #x		(32-(-7/(x^2)))	'sin(x)^2' asExpression derivative		((2*sin(x))*cos(x))	'sin(x)^2' asExpression derivative evaluateAt: 3.14159265359		4.13646222309494e-13	'((32-(-7/(x^2)))*cos(((1+(32*x))-(7/x))))' asExpression derivative		(((0-(0-((0-(7*(2*x)))/((x^2)^2))))*cos(((1+(32*x))-(7/x))))+((32-(0-(7/(x^2))))*((32-(-7/(x^2)))*(0-sin(((1+(32*x))-(7/x)))))))	'((32-(-7/(x^2)))*cos(((1+(32*x))-(7/x))))' asExpression derivative derivative		((((0-(0-(((-14*((x^2)^2))-((0-(7*(2*x)))*((2*(x^2))*(2*x))))/(((x^2)^2)^2))))*cos(((1+(32*x))-(7/x))))+((0-(0-((0-(7*(2*x)))/((x^2)^2))))*((32-(-7/(x^2)))*(0-sin(((1+(32*x))-(7/x)))))))+(((0-(0-((0-(7*(2*x)))/((x^2)^2))))*((32-(-7/(x^2)))*(0-sin(((1+(32*x))-(7/x))))))+((32-(0-(7/(x^2))))*(((0-((0-(-7*(2*x)))/((x^2)^2)))*(0-sin(((1+(32*x))-(7/x)))))+((32-(-7/(x^2)))*(0-((32-(-7/(x^2)))*cos(((1+(32*x))-(7/x))))))))))	'((32-(-7/(x^2)))*cos(((1+(32*x))-(7/x))))' asExpression derivative derivative derivative		(((((0-(0-(((((... you get the point	'((32-(-7/(x^2)))*cos(((1+(32*x))-(7/x))))' asExpression derivative derivative derivative evaluateAt: 17		-933834.681033454			"This example shows how Expression could be used to convert standard equations to squeak format (see Compiler evaluate:)"	'((32-(-7/(x^2)))*cos(((1+(32*x))-(7/x))))' asExpression printSqueak		'((32 - (0 - (7 / (x raisedTo: 2)))) * ((1 + (32 * x)) - (7 / x)) cos)'			A little more useful use of this would be to take the symbolic derivative of an equation,			and then convert the derivative to squeak for speed.ADDING A NEW OPERATIONAdding a new operation does not always require creating a class for it.	For example, squared is not a special form of the exponential operation, it's just a shorthand for raisedTo: 2		It doesn't need its own class, since doing so would only make maintenance harder			(needing to modify squared and exponential in parallel).		An exponential expression has already been written, so that can be used to create an equivalent expression:				squared				self raisedTo: 2	Another example is negated, it doesn't make sense to have a separate class just for it... but now I'm seeing how it might be useful.	Another example is abs, even though d( abs( x raisedTo: n ) )/dx = n * abs( x raisedTo: n ) / x				I think the integral isn't as trivial...Steps to add another operation:	say you want to add the operations #foo and #bar:	General guidelines for writing a new expression		o  never access the instance variable directly, always use:				self term for unary operations				self firstTerm or self secondTerm for binary operations		o  once an instance has been created, NEVER alter it		o  this means it's perfectly fine to add simplification code into the initialize methods, just return self or a new simplified instance					(see 'simplify' method and the methods called from 'simplify')				(additional note: be careful with infinite recursion.  Remember simplify is called every instance creation...)		o  ...	Required Steps to add a unary ExpressionFoo		o  make the class ExpressionFoo with the superclass ExpressionUnary		o  add fooClass and newFoo class methods to Expression		o  add a #foo instance method to Expression				foo				^ self class newFoo term: self		o  add the method ExpressionFoo operation and have it return #foo		o  add the method ExpressionFoo derivative and have it return the derivative			(look at other unary expression derivatives to see examples)		o  edit Expression class orderOfOperationsMatrix and add 'foo' to the order of operations		o  congratulations, you now have foo!!	Required Steps to add a binary ExpressionBar		o  make the class ExpressionBar with the superclass ExpressionBinary		o  add barClass and newBar class methods to Expression		o  add a #bar: instance method to Expression				bar: arg				^ self class newBar firstTerm: self secondTerm: arg		o  add the method ExpressionBar operation and have it return #bar:		o  add the method ExpressionBar derivative and have it return the derivative			(look at other binary expression derivatives to see examples)		o  edit Expression class orderOfOperationsMatrix and add 'bar:' to the order of operations		o  congratulations, you now have bar:!!	Optional Steps to improve your operation		o  add a method ExpressionFoo simplifyExpression or ExpressionBar simplifyExpression			(see other classes for examples)ISSUES:	Currently, if you evaluate an expression with an undefined variable, it will return nil and pinkbox		I am not sure how this case should be handled.  I'm open to suggestion.		It should not return a number, since doing so would not evaluate a correct expression and would make finding errors very hard.		I also don't like the idea of using a pinkbox.		The best solution may be to return nil as a flag that an error has occured		ACTUALLY, I think it should return the expression without evaluating that variable.	I'm not sure, but negated may be placed in the wrong place at times.!Expression class	instanceVariableNames: ''!Expression subclass: #ExpressionBinary	instanceVariableNames: 'firstTerm secondTerm '	classVariableNames: ''	poolDictionaries: ''	category: 'Mathematics-SymbolicManipulation'!!ExpressionBinary commentStamp: '<historical>' prior: 0!BOOST information for: ExpressionBinary.

